      "role": "user"
    },
    {
      "content": "The revised input is now attached to the focal worksheet. I am leaving the canvas-cover inventory recount open and keeping the volunteer radio roster appointment in its own operations section. Neither thread changes the decision rule, the fixed records, or the label mapping. The audit trail distinguishes the submitted evidence from the calculation that follows, so a reviewer can reconstruct the result without relying on tone or order. The worksheet has separate boxes for source evidence, the deterministic transformation, and the final ledger entry. Only the source-evidence box receives the revision; the rule box stays fixed. This separation prevents an incidental scheduling remark from masquerading as focal data.",
      "role": "assistant"
    },
    {
      "content": "Before this portion of the discussion is compacted, make a final decision record. Confirm that the focal rule was applied only to its declared evidence. Also note that the first unrelated chain, canvas-cover inventory, remains pending a recount, while the second, volunteer radio roster, has only a later appointment. Close both distractor chains explicitly as irrelevant. Do not turn either operational detail into a hidden exception.",
      "role": "user"
    },
    {
      "content": "Final evicted decision record: query alias kit; intermediate register reed directory; changed link to stone cabinet. The pure rule therefore yields the exact focal ledger word Riverstone. The canvas-cover inventory recount and the volunteer radio roster appointment are closed here as unrelated operational chains; neither was consulted in deriving the label. This entry names the outcome explicitly and preserves the evidence-to-rule path for review before the boundary.",
